@@ -2708,3 +2708,164 @@ register:
         doc: f8853
         locator: Form 8853 (2025), line 25, "Per diem limitation. Subtract line 24 from line 23"
       status: Planning lane lows-inc (BUG-0917), drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_required_to_file
+      text_at_issue: 'i8615 p. 1, Who Must File, condition 2: "The child is required to file a tax return for 2025." A blind
+        child, or a dependent child with little income, may file without being required to, and the tool does not ask
+        whether the filer is required to file. An earlier design refused a blind dependent whose filing requirement was
+        unsettled.'
+      readings:
+      - Ask whether the child is required to file (or refuse a blind dependent, whose Chart B thresholds differ), and leave
+        Form 8615 out when the child is not.
+      - Compute Form 8615 whenever conditions 1 and 3 to 5 hold, without testing condition 2.
+      chosen: The second (f8615.applies has no line for condition 2).
+      reason: 'The two readings give the same return. Chart B (i1040gi p. 10) requires a dependent to file when gross income is
+        more than the larger of $1,350 or earned income (up to $15,300) plus $450, and a blind dependent when it is more than
+        the larger of $3,350 or earned income plus $2,450: in both cases exactly the dependent''s standard deduction, the
+        blind addition included (i1040gi pp. 34 and 35). A single filer who is not a dependent must file when gross income
+        is at least the standard deduction ($15,750, or $17,750 if blind). A child not required to file therefore has gross
+        income, and so adjusted gross income, no more than the standard deduction, taxable income zero, Form 8615 line 4
+        zero, line 5 zero, and line 18 equal to line 17, the tax figured in the normal manner. A child required to file only
+        because of another tax (Chart C) meets condition 2 and is computed under either reading.'
+      cite:
+        doc: i8615
+        locator: 'p. 1, Who Must File, "2. The child is required to file a tax return for 2025"; i1040gi p. 10, Chart B—For
+          Children and Other Dependents'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16 (the scout's section 0.2 item 3, re-derived); for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_earned
+      text_at_issue: 'i8615 p. 2, Earned income: "earned income also includes a reasonable allowance for compensation for
+        personal services, but not more than 30% of the child’s share of the net profits from that trade or business", "In
+        that case, earned income is generally the total of the amounts reported on Form 1040 or 1040-NR, line 1z, and
+        Schedule 1 (Form 1040), lines 3 and 6", and a taxable distribution from a qualified disability trust is earned income.'
+      readings:
+      - Earned income is Form 1040 line 1z plus Schedule 1 line 3 (a loss taken as zero), the "generally" of the text.
+      - Ask for the 30% allowance where capital is a material income-producing factor, and for qualified disability trust
+        distributions.
+      chosen: The first (f8615.earned). Schedule 1 line 6 (farm income) is refused through scope.schedule1.
+      reason: The text gives the sum as the general rule and the alternatives as exceptions a child rarely has; neither has a
+        question in the tool. A child with a business in which capital matters, or with a qualified disability trust, is
+        computed by the general rule.
+      cite:
+        doc: i8615
+        locator: 'p. 2, Earned income'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_alternate_worksheet
+      text_at_issue: 'i8615 p. 2: "But use the Alternate Worksheet for Form 8615, Line 1 ... if the child: Files Form 2555,
+        Has a net loss from self-employment, or Claims a net operating loss deduction." The Alternate Worksheet adds the
+        loss and the deduction back (line B) and subtracts earned income with a loss taken as zero (line D).'
+      readings:
+      - Encode the two worksheets separately, chosen by the three conditions.
+      - Encode one expression, Form 1040 line 9 less line 1z, Schedule 1 line 3 as entered, Schedule 1 line 18 and Schedule 1
+        line 8a (a negative amount), which equals each worksheet where it applies.
+      chosen: The second (f8615.uiw). Form 2555 is refused elsewhere (sa.excluded_income).
+      reason: 'With a Schedule C loss L and no deduction, the Alternate Worksheet gives line 9 + L − 1z − 0 − line 18, the same
+        as line 9 − 1z − (−L) − line 18; with a net operating loss deduction N, line 9 already includes −N (Schedule 1 line
+        8a) and the Alternate Worksheet adds N back, as subtracting line 8a does; with neither, the expression is the Child''s
+        Unearned Income Worksheet. Losses from self-employment outside Schedule C (a partnership) are not earned income by the
+        general rule (amb.2025.f8615_earned).'
+      cite:
+        doc: i8615
+        locator: 'p. 2, Alternate Worksheet for Form 8615, Line 1; p. 3, Child’s Unearned Income Worksheet—Line 1'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_line1_branch
+      text_at_issue: 'i8615 p. 2, Line 1: "If the child had no earned income (defined below), enter the child’s adjusted gross
+        income from Form 1040 or 1040-NR, line 11b. If the child had earned income, use the Child''s Unearned Income
+        Worksheet ... But use the Alternate Worksheet ... if the child ... Has a net loss from self-employment".'
+      readings:
+      - A child with no earned income enters adjusted gross income, even with a net loss from self-employment.
+      - A child with a net loss from self-employment uses the Alternate Worksheet even with no other earned income.
+      chosen: The first (f8615.line1 chooses on f8615.earned, a loss taken as zero).
+      reason: The text's first sentence is unconditioned and the Alternate Worksheet is introduced as an exception to the
+        earned-income branch ("If the child had earned income ... But use"). The readings differ only for a child whose only
+        work is a business at a loss (the first reading's line 1 is lower by the loss), or who has adjustments other than the
+        early withdrawal penalty and no earned income; a dependent can rarely have those (student loan interest and the HSA
+        deduction are not allowed to a dependent, and an IRA deduction needs compensation).
+      cite:
+        doc: i8615
+        locator: 'p. 2, Line 1'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_half_support
+      text_at_issue: 'i8615 p. 1, Who Must File, condition 3: "didn’t have earned income that was more than half of the
+        child’s support". The tool asks i8863 line 7''s question, "less than one-half of your support" (person.edu_half_support).'
+      readings:
+      - Reuse the Form 8863 question; a child whose earned income is exactly half of support answers No and is treated as not
+        meeting condition 3.
+      - Ask a separate question with the Form 8615 wording.
+      chosen: The first (f8615.kid_count reads person.edu_kid_age). One fact, one question.
+      reason: The two tests differ only at exactly one-half, a boundary a filer cannot place to the dollar; the same age, student
+        and parent facts are already asked for Form 8863.
+      cite:
+        doc: i8615
+        locator: 'p. 1, Who Must File, condition 3'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16 (the scout's O7, taken under delegation); for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_line10_amt
+      text_at_issue: 'Form 8615 (2025), line 10: "Enter the parent’s tax from Form 1040 or 1040-NR, line 16, minus any
+        alternative minimum tax." i8615 p. 8, Line 10, says only "Enter on line 10 the amount from Form 1040, 1040-SR, or
+        1040-NR, line 16. Don’t include the tax, if any, from Form 4972 or Form 8814 or any tax from recapture of an
+        education credit."'
+      readings:
+      - Subtract the parent's alternative minimum tax from line 16.
+      - Enter line 16 as the instructions say.
+      chosen: The second (the question asks for line 16 without Forms 4972 and 8814 and an education credit recapture).
+      reason: Form 1040 line 16 holds no alternative minimum tax, which is on Schedule 2 line 2; for a Form 1040 parent the two
+        readings give the same amount. The words read as a carry-over for Form 1040-NR or earlier layouts.
+      cite:
+        doc: f8615
+        locator: 'Form 8615 (2025), line 10'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_line11_sign
+      text_at_issue: 'Form 8615 (2025), line 11: "Subtract line 10 from line 9 and enter the result." No floor is printed.'
+      readings:
+      - Line 11 is the signed difference.
+      - Line 11 is not less than zero.
+      chosen: The first (f8615.line11 is subtract).
+      reason: Line 9 taxes more income than line 10 at the same status and rates, so line 11 is negative only when the parent's
+        line 16 holds something line 9 does not (a figure entered wrongly, or a tax the instructions say to leave out). The
+        larger of lines 16 and 17 on line 18 keeps the child's tax at least the tax at the child's own rate.
+      cite:
+        doc: f8615
+        locator: 'Form 8615 (2025), line 11'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
+    - id: amb.2025.f8615_ratio_places
+      text_at_issue: 'Form 8615 line 12b and the Line 5 Worksheets: "Enter the result as a decimal (rounded to at least three
+        places)"; i8615 p. 8 shows 0.333 for $3,000 / $9,000.'
+      readings:
+      - Round each ratio half-up to exactly three places.
+      - Keep the ratio at full precision.
+      chosen: The first (the prorate atom with places 3), as amb.2025.edu_ratio_places reads the same words; the Persimmon
+        example's 0.333 agrees.
+      reason: The owner's ruling on the same words for Forms 8863 and the student loan interest worksheet.
+      cite:
+        doc: i8615
+        locator: 'p. 8, Lines 12a and 12b, "The decimal on line 12b is 0.333, figured as follows and rounded to three places"'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; follows amb.2025.edu_ratio_places.
+    - id: amb.2025.amt_kiddie_child
+      text_at_issue: 'Rev. Proc. 2024-40 section 3.12: "for a child to whom the § 1(g) “kiddie tax” applies, the exemption
+        amount ... may not exceed the sum of (1) the child’s earned income for the taxable year, plus (2) $9,550." The
+        archived i6251 and f6251 say nothing of the cap.'
+      readings:
+      - A child who meets the age, parent and joint-return conditions of section 1(g)(2) (Form 8615 Who Must File conditions 3
+        to 5), whatever the amount of unearned income.
+      - Only a child who files Form 8615 (unearned income over $2,700).
+      chosen: The first (s2.amt_kiddie reads f8615.child_1g).
+      reason: 'Section 1(g)(2) says when the subsection "shall apply to any child" by age, parent and joint return; the amount of
+        net unearned income decides how much tax it imposes, not whether it applies [inferred: the Code is not archived]. The
+        readings differ only for a child with $2,700 or less of unearned income whose line 4 still exceeds earned income plus
+        $9,550, which needs alternative minimum tax adjustments of several thousand dollars.'
+      cite:
+        doc: rp-2024-40
+        locator: 'section 3.12'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner (the Code text is not archived).
+    - id: amb.2025.amt_kiddie_earned
+      text_at_issue: 'Rev. Proc. 2024-40 section 3.12 caps the exemption at "the child’s earned income for the taxable year,
+        plus (2) $9,550" without defining earned income.'
+      readings:
+      - The Form 8615 earned income (f8615.earned, Form 1040 line 1z and Schedule 1 line 3 with a loss as zero).
+      - Earned income as section 911(d)(2) defines it (wages and other pay for personal services), which the Code cross-refers
+        to [inferred].
+      chosen: The first.
+      reason: The two definitions agree for wages and a profitable sole proprietorship; they could differ for a business in which
+        capital is material (the 30% rule) or a partner's earnings, which the tool takes by the general rule in both places.
+      cite:
+        doc: rp-2024-40
+        locator: 'section 3.12'
+      status: Planning lane D5-8615, drafted 2026-11-16; for the owner.
